--TEST--
`Core\Http\Response::text` answers a `tainted string` and `::status` an ordinary `int`, so a plain `string` binding refuses the body and nothing refuses the code — ADR 0024 § 1, ADR 0074 § 6
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The claim is *a bound asserted on both sides*: the two readers of one value
// are bound to the two types, and only the body's binding is refused. A
// `text()` that quietly lost the qualifier passes the second half of this and
// fails the first; a `status()` that gained one fails the other way round.
//
// A reply is bytes another host chose, and pinning an address settles where
// they came from rather than what is in them — ADR 0024 § 1's roster names
// this reader for that reason.
Core\Http\Target $pinned = Core\Http::allowUrl("http://93.184.216.34/ok");
Core\Http\Response $r = Core\Http\Client::get($pinned);

// Accepted: three digits carry nothing a sink can misread.
int $status = $r->status();
echo $status as string, "\n";

// Accepted: the qualifier is what the value is, and a `tainted string` is
// where it can be held.
tainted string $held = $r->text();
echo $held, "\n";

// Refused: narrowing through an assignment is the one direction the qualifier
// never goes, and there is no generic launderer to put in front of it.
string $body = $r->text();
echo $body, "\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `tainted string`
   --> case.nvs:24:16
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
